WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.404
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:06.090
My name is Yangbing Li.

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:09.400
I'm a Senior Vice President and
General Manager at VMware.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.870
I lead what we call the storied and
the availability business unit here.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.800
So in the beginning of 2014,
I was making a career

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.990
transition moving from China back
to Palo Alto, our headquarters.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:26.900
Our head of talent development and
HR suggested

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.590
that I consider SEP to open up my horizon,

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:36.740
expand my thinking about what's
possible out there, build new networks.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:42.330
I went into SEP trying to expand my
understanding of business leadership.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.850
I've been in a technical leadership
role for many, many years.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:49.810
So I was very interested in thinking
about how do I expand my horizon

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:52.260
from technology to business leadership?

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.130
Within a year coming back from SEP,
I was given the opportunity to change

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:00.970
from VP of engineering role to a GM role,

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:05.450
essentially leading up and
coming business that we have in storage.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:12.120
Reflecting on what I learned in SEP,
one thing that really opened my eyes was

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:17.870
there's just so much beyond technology and
engineering in product.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:22.936
When you run a business, so
how do you maintain a focus

00:01:22.936 --> 00:01:27.552
of creating a sense of
innovation incubation for

00:01:27.552 --> 00:01:32.066
new development areas and
new revenue stream?

00:01:32.066 --> 00:01:36.369
But in the meantime, continue to
support and grow existing businesses

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:40.540
constantly a leadership struggle but
in the innovator's dilemma.

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:47.135
I constantly draw to my SEP language for
ideas and inspirations.

00:01:47.135 --> 00:01:51.140
SEP gave me a lot of good framework,

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.120
lots of powerful languages to let me

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:00.390
think about the business problems
that I face today or ongoing basis.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:05.430
In the past few years we've been
on this what we initially consider

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:10.925
impossible goal of a building a billion
dollar business here in my team.

00:02:10.925 --> 00:02:13.193
And we just celebrated that milestone.

00:02:13.193 --> 00:02:18.869
SEP, I really see it as a great catalyst

00:02:18.869 --> 00:02:24.725
in the evolutions of myself as a leader.

